<Project>

  <!--
    Repo-wide NuGet restore policy.

    Every PackageReference in this repo is pinned to an exact version, and restore writes a
    packages.lock.json next to each project recording the full transitive closure with content
    hashes. Together these mean a restore resolves to the same bytes on every machine and in CI,
    and any drift — a re-published package, a transitive bump, an unpinned addition — fails the
    restore instead of silently changing the build.

    This is the substantive supply-chain control for the repo: it covers all ~27 packages rather
    than singling out one dependency for special treatment. It is also why vendoring a package is
    not required merely to freeze it (see #70 for that decision, and third_party/oxyplot-avalonia
    for the case where vendoring IS warranted — no compatible NuGet release exists at all).

    IMPORTANT: RestorePackagesWithLockFile only GENERATES the lock file. On its own, a restore that
    encounters drift quietly REGENERATES it instead of failing, which defeats the purpose. Locked
    mode is what turns the lock file into a gate, so RestoreLockedMode is enabled below whenever an
    automated build is detected.

    Locally it stays off, because it has to: adding or bumping a package legitimately changes the
    closure, so a developer restores normally to update the lock file and commits it alongside the
    change. To rehearse the gate by hand, pass -p:ContinuousIntegrationBuild=true (or restore with
    the locked-mode flag).

    The gate is live: .github/workflows/build.yml restores and builds every head with CI set, so a
    drifted lock file fails the run (#76 / #77).

    DO NOT COMMIT A LOCK FILE PRODUCED BY AN EXPLICIT RID RESTORE (the -r flag). A RID-specific restore
    REWRITES the lock file to hold only that RID, dropping the others:

        dotnet restore Daqifi.Avalonia.Desktop -r win-x64
        -> lock targets go from  net10.0 + linux-x64, osx-arm64, osx-x64, win-x64
                           to    net10.0 + win-x64

    Committing that breaks CI on the very next run, because the pruned file no longer matches the
    project's declared RuntimeIdentifiers:

        error NU1004: The project's runtime identifiers have changed from.
        Project's runtime identifiers: linux-x64;osx-arm64;osx-x64;win-x64,
        lock file's runtime identifiers win-x64.

    This is easy to do by accident — publishing, or building the Windows head from WSL, both use an
    explicit RID — and one such restore rewrote 1004 lines across three lock files, which reads as
    innocuous churn in a diff. If `git status` shows a packages.lock.json modified after a RID
    build, revert it; do not stage it. Restoring without -r (or with the force-evaluate flag) puts the full
    RID set back. The build workflow also asserts the tree is unchanged after restore, so this
    cannot land silently (#85).

    NOTE: third_party/oxyplot-avalonia has its own Directory.Build.props and MSBuild stops at the
    first one it finds walking up, so this file is NOT inherited there — the property is set in
    that file too. Keep them in step.

    NOTE: tools/parity-audit/WpfCapture opts OUT (see its csproj). It ProjectReferences the
    sibling daqifi-desktop repo, so a lock file there would encode another repo's closure and
    go stale on that repo's package bumps, with no PR here able to refresh it. Six of the seven
    projects carry lock files; that one is the deliberate exception.
  -->
  <!--
    The application's marketing version, in ONE place.

    Four projects need it and each used to spell "3.3.0" out for itself: the shared library
    (<Version>, which is what the in-app version text and the Sentry release are read from — see
    Daqifi.Desktop.Common.AppVersion), the Desktop head (<Version>), and the store display
    versions on the Android and iOS heads (<ApplicationDisplayVersion>, which becomes
    android:versionName and CFBundleShortVersionString respectively).

    Four hand-maintained copies of a number that must agree is a silent-drift hazard, and drift
    here has a specific cost: the version a crash is tagged with in Sentry comes from the shared
    library, while the version a user reports comes from the store listing. If those two ever
    disagree, every triage conversation starts from a false premise (#126 is what that looks
    like in practice). Bump this one property to cut a release.

    Deliberately NOT single-sourced here: the per-store build numbers (<ApplicationVersion> —
    Android versionCode, iOS CFBundleVersion). Those are per-store counters on their own
    schedule; Play requires a unique increasing code for every upload, including re-uploads of
    an unchanged marketing version, so tying them together would be wrong.

    NOTE: third_party/oxyplot-avalonia has its own Directory.Build.props and does not inherit
    this file. That is correct — a vendored library keeps its own upstream version.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <DaqifiAppVersion>3.3.0</DaqifiAppVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <!-- Fail on drift in automated builds, stay permissive for local development. `CI` is set by
         GitHub Actions (and most other runners) automatically; ContinuousIntegrationBuild is the
         MSBuild-native switch and lets a developer rehearse the gate on demand. -->
    <RestoreLockedMode Condition="'$(ContinuousIntegrationBuild)' == 'true' Or '$(CI)' == 'true'">true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
